<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chartism</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1838">1838</a>–<a href="/years/1857">1857</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Chartist movement</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chartism was a working-class movement for political reform in the United Kingdom, named after the People's Charter of 1838 and active until 1857. The Charter demanded a vote for every man aged 21 or over, the secret ballot, payment of members of parliament and no property qualification for them, equal constituencies and annual parliaments. Parliament rejected its mass petitions of 1839 and 1842, the second signed by over three million people, and the Newport Rising of 1839 was put down. After the movement died out, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> adopted all its demands except annual parliaments.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chartism.jpg" alt="Chartism" width="320" height="228" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William Edward Kilburn · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chartist_meeting_on_Kennington_Common_by_William_Edward_Kilburn_1848_-_restoration1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
